  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 AM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nThe latest RAP analysis and satellite and radar imagery show a \nsplit flow pattern across North America, characterized by \nshortwave energy traversing the central Plains that is \nundercutting a broad area of ridging positioned over Canada. \nCloser to home, a sprawling area of strong surface high pressure \ncentered over northern Ontario continues to supply a cool and dry \nairmass into the western Great Lakes. Precipitable water values \nhover around 70 percent of normal as a result. Temperatures aloft \nare sufficiently cold to support the development of lake clouds \nover northeast Wisconsin courtesy of persistent northeast winds, \nbut regional radar imagery remains free of precipitation echoes \nthis morning. Upstream, an upper-level low pressure system \nsituated over the Plains is expected to migrate eastward toward \nthe Great Lakes from late tonight into Tuesday, bringing periods \nof mid and high-level clouds over the local area. \n\nAn large layer of dry air situated between 875mb and 700mb will \nexist across the region today and tonight. While some high- \nresolution guidance attempts to bring light sprinkles or showers \ninto central Wisconsin late this afternoon and evening in \nassociation with the approaching upper low, confidence remains \nvery low that any precipitation will reach the surface. \nDaytime high temperatures will remain in the low to mid 60s today\nand Tuesday.\n\nWith the cool Canadian airmass entrenched and persistent \nnortheasterly flow, overnight low temperatures are forecast to \ndrop into the mid to upper 30s across sheltered and low-lying \nareas of far northern Wisconsin late tonight and again on Tuesday \nnight. Widespread mid and high- level cloud cover streaming in \nahead of the approaching upper- level low creates some uncertainty\nregarding optimal radiational cooling conditions. \n\nQuiet weather will remain across the region as the upper-level \nlow slowly drifts from the central Great Lakes toward the East \nCoast Wednesday through Friday. In its wake, surface high pressure\nwill remain present over the western Great Lakes and effectively \nsuppress any weak shortwave energy attempting to advance into the \nregion on Thursday and Saturday. Precipitation chances will remain under\n10 percent through Saturday. The next potential for precipitation\narrives late Saturday night into Sunday as a broad trough begins \nto slide toward the central United States, providing a slight 20 \npercent chance of rain showers to close out the forecast period.\nTemps will undergo a steady warming trend late in the week, with \nhigh temperatures rebounding from the 60s into the lower 70s by \nthe weekend.",
